From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Christian=20F=C3=BCrst?= <christian.fuerst@ludofact.de>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 12:34:54 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat: HTTP entry point with per-request auth

`mcp-server-azuredevops-http <organization>` serves MCP over stateless
Streamable HTTP for deployment behind a gateway that authenticates each
caller. The server holds no credential: every POST must carry the caller's
Entra access token for Azure DevOps as a bearer token, and each request
gets a fresh McpServer whose token and connection providers return that
token only, so one caller's token is never used for another's tool call.
Requests without a bearer token get 401 before any tool runs.

A body over 4 MiB gets 413. The SDK would otherwise buffer a body of any
size, and any bearer string passes the check above.

On SIGTERM the server stops listening and closes connections as they go
idle. A connection that is still open, such as a gateway's pooled one,
gets Connection: close on its next response. Without that it keeps taking
requests, and the process never exits.

An option given twice takes its last value, because a container runner's
arguments follow the image's own. An unknown domain stops the server,
where upstream would enable every domain, write tools included.

orgName moves from index.ts to organization.ts, because importing index.ts
starts the stdio server; the stdio entry point is otherwise unchanged.

Stateless mode cannot send requests back to the client, so tools that
elicit a missing project or team fail instead of asking.

jest.config.cjs takes the generic relative-import mapper that upstream main
already uses: the per-module list cannot resolve the new modules, and its
index.js rule rewrites the SDK's own ./index.js to a file that does not exist.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
